# Layout of the file `cargo xtask compose-secrets` writes to deploy/compose/.env.
# This file has NO real values; do not put secrets into it. Run the command instead.
CRAFTCLOUD_DOMAIN=cloud.example.org
CRAFTCLOUD_PUBLIC_URL=https://cloud.example.org
CRAFTCLOUD_HTTP_PORT=80
CRAFTCLOUD_HTTPS_PORT=443
CRAFTCLOUD_TLS_MODE=acme
# Optional: image name (default craftcloud:local) and the proxy-side network.
# CRAFTCLOUD_IMAGE=craftcloud:local
# CRAFTCLOUD_FRONTEND_SUBNET=172.29.205.0/24

# Generated: CC_SECRET_DB_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD, CC_SECRET_DB_OWNER_PASSWORD,
# CC_SECRET_DB_RUNTIME_PASSWORD, CC_SECRET_BOOTSTRAP, CC_SECRET_METRICS_TOKEN,
# CC_SECRET_TWO_FACTOR_KEY (Base64 of 32 random bytes; keep a separate copy, backups exclude it).
# The database URLs are built from the role passwords by secrets-init.sh.

# FigCraft live companion (default companion, enabled by the installer): always add
# -f compose.figcraft.yaml after compose.yaml, e.g.
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.figcraft.yaml up -d
# The installer writes CRAFTCLOUD_FIGCRAFT=enabled|disabled, creates the key file and sets
# the variables below. For plain `docker compose` store a separately generated
# 64-character random hex key in a private host file (mode 0600).
# CRAFTCLOUD_FIGCRAFT=enabled
# CRAFTCLOUD_FIGCRAFT_SECRET_FILE=./secrets/figcraft.key
# CRAFTCLOUD_FIGCRAFT_PUBLIC_URL=wss://cloud.example.org/figcraft/
# CRAFTCLOUD_FIGCRAFT_IMAGE is an alias for CRAFTCLOUD_IMAGE and works for one more release (the FigCraft
# server is part of the CraftCloud image). Leave it unset.
